**Compare**:
- 5 V, 100 mA limit, R1 = 220 Ω: wants 5 / 220 ≈ 22.7 mA < 100 mA → CV, shows 5.00 V, 0.023 A;
- 10 mA limit, R1 = 220 Ω: wants 22.7 mA > 10 mA → CC, voltage = 10 mA × 220 Ω = **2.20 V**;
- 12 V set, 10 mA limit, R1 = 470 Ω: wants 25.5 mA > 10 mA → still CC, voltage = 10 mA × 470 Ω = **4.70 V**, current still **10 mA**;
- 20 mA limit: wants 25.5 mA > 20 mA → still CC, voltage = 20 mA × 470 Ω = **9.4 V**;
- limit back to 100 mA: wants 25.5 mA < 100 mA → CV, 12 V, current ≈ **25.5 mA**.
The largest load this 10 mA source can drive is 12 V / 10 mA = 1.2 kΩ; above that the voltage hits the ceiling and the supply goes back to CV.
